    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We undertake a multidisciplinary investigation of asphalt distribution in the Anacacho Limestone to decipher potential roles of fractures and faults on secondary hydrocarbon migration. Field relationships between fractures, faults, and asphalt are evaluated at an asphaltic limestone mine near Uvalde, TX. Based upon their distributions and geometries, we infer that normal faults provided vertical flow paths through the Anacacho Limestone while strata-bound fractures enhanced its permeability. Variograms calculated from 75 subsurface measurements indicate that the asphalt concentration is anisotropically correlated and that the maximum range points similarly to the mean strike of the fractures and faults, ~52° northeast. A globally positioned laser rangefinder is used to measure faults and stratigraphic contacts within the mine. That data is then combined with lithologic descriptions from surrounding subsurface wells to construct a digital three dimensional model of the Anacacho Limestone. When the model is populated with asphalt concentration estimated with an ordinary block kriging algorithm, we find that the two largest normal fault zones align within the central axis of an isosurface enclosing high values. The three dimensional model also provides a framework to numerically simulate secondary hydrocarbon migration. Simulation parameters are adjusted within physically realistic ranges to produce an oil saturation field in agreement with asphalt concentration estimates. Our results indicate that oil entered the Anacacho Limestone through normal faults, that regional aquifer flow impacted oil migration, and that fractures increased the horizontal permeability of the formation by an order of magnitude along their strike direction.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
